\title{The Halting Problem for Self-Modifying Code:\\ Undecidable, but Not Strictly Harder}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop, as a single linear chain of results, the theory of the halting problem for \emph{self-modifying} computation---machines whose transition rule may rewrite the running program mid-execution. Starting from Lawvere's fixed-point theorem, we descend through Cantor's diagonal argument to a purely operational statement that no program can realize the ``contrarian'' (anti-diagonal) behavior. We then introduce a self-modifying machine model, prove a step-exact simulation theorem showing that any self-modifying machine is faithfully mimicked by an ordinary fixed-program machine over an enlarged state space (``code becomes data''), and use it to establish that the halting problems for self-modifying and fixed-program machines are \emph{many-one equivalent}. This corrects a common informal claim: self-modification does \textbf{not} make halting strictly harder; it introduces no higher Turing degree. The genuine phenomenon is self-reference. We prove a self-referential halting theorem---any system able to build the contrarian program defeats every candidate total halting decider---and derive two consequences: a \emph{virus paradox} (no total detector decides self-halting behavior everywhere) and an \emph{alignment obstruction} (no total monitor can correctly certify a non-trivial self-referential behavioral property for every program).
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Self-modifying code---a program that rewrites its own instructions while executing---has an aura of danger and mystery. Metamorphic viruses, just-in-time compilers, genetic-programming systems, and increasingly self-editing machine-learning pipelines all blur the classical boundary between program and data. A recurring piece of folklore holds that predicting the behavior of such programs must be \emph{strictly harder} than the classical halting problem: if a program can become something else while you are analyzing it, surely no analysis can keep up.

This paper subjects that intuition to precise scrutiny and finds it wrong. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A clean, abstract derivation of the diagonal machinery, from Lawvere's fixed-point theorem down to an operational ``no contrarian program'' lemma (Section~\ref{sec:engine}).
  \item A formal self-modifying machine model with a program that may change every step, and a faithful simulation theorem (Sections~\ref{sec:model}--\ref{sec:sim}).
  \item A proof that the self-modifying and fixed-program halting problems are many-one equivalent (Section~\ref{sec:equiv}), correcting the ``strictly harder'' folklore.
  \item A self-referential halting theorem and two applications---the virus paradox and an alignment obstruction (Sections~\ref{sec:selfref}--\ref{sec:apps}).
\end{enumerate}

The through-line is that self-modification and self-reference are distinct phenomena with opposite morals. Self-modification is \emph{cheap}: it can always be flattened into ordinary memory manipulation, adding no computational power. Self-reference is \emph{fatal}: any system able to describe and act against its own analyzers admits a contrarian that no analyzer survives.

The undecidability of the classical halting problem is due to Turing (1936); Cantor's diagonal argument (1891) is its set-theoretic ancestor, and Lawvere's fixed-point theorem (1969) is the categorical distillation unifying them, since all three are the single observation that a fixed-point-free endomap obstructs any surjection onto a function space. Rice's theorem (1953) extends undecidability to every non-trivial behavioral property. Our contribution is not a new undecidability phenomenon but a precise placement of self-modifying computation relative to this classical landscape, with an explicit derivation of the alignment-relevant corollaries.

\section{Preliminaries and Notation}

We work constructively over arbitrary types. We write $\Bool = \{\true, \false\}$ with negation $\lnot$, and use $\Opt X = \{\none\} \cup \{\some\,x : x \in X\}$ to model partial results, with $\none$ denoting ``halted.'' A function $g$ is \emph{surjective} if every element of its codomain is a value of $g$.

For predicates $A : \alpha \to \mathrm{Prop}$ and $B : \beta \to \mathrm{Prop}$, we say $A$ \emph{many-one reduces} to $B$, written $A \lemred B$, if there is a total function $f : \alpha \to \beta$ with $A(x) \iff B(f(x))$ for all $x$. If $A \lemred B$ and $B \lemred A$ we call $A$ and $B$ \emph{many-one equivalent}.

\section{The Diagonalization Engine}\label{sec:engine}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere]\label{thm:lawvere}
Let $A$ and $B$ be types and let $g : A \to (A \to B)$ be surjective. Then every self-map $f : B \to B$ has a fixed point: there exists $b \in B$ with $f(b) = b$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Consider $x \mapsto f(g(x)(x))$. By surjectivity it equals $g(a)$ for some $a$. Evaluating at $a$ gives $g(a)(a) = f(g(a)(a))$, so $b := g(a)(a)$ is a fixed point of $f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor, Boolean form]\label{thm:cantor}
For any type $A$, no map $g : A \to (A \to \Bool)$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The negation $\lnot : \Bool \to \Bool$ has no fixed point ($\lnot b \ne b$). If $g$ were surjective, Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere} would produce a fixed point of $\lnot$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Interpret a \emph{behavior} as a map $\mathrm{beh} : \Prog \to (\Prog \to \Bool)$; the contrarian behavior is $q \mapsto \lnot\,\mathrm{beh}(q)(q)$.

\begin{theorem}[No contrarian behavior]\label{thm:contra}
No program $p_0$ satisfies $\mathrm{beh}(p_0) = \big(q \mapsto \lnot\,\mathrm{beh}(q)(q)\big)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Evaluating such an equality at $p_0$ yields $\mathrm{beh}(p_0)(p_0) = \lnot\,\mathrm{beh}(p_0)(p_0)$---a Boolean equal to its own negation, impossible.
\end{proof}

\section{A Self-Modifying Machine Model}\label{sec:model}

\begin{definition}[Self-modifying machine]
Fix types $P$ (programs) and $S$ (states). A \emph{self-modifying machine} (SMM) is a transition function $\step : P \to S \to \Opt(P \times S)$. Given $(p,s)$, $\step(p,s)$ is either $\none$ (halt) or $\some(p',s')$, delivering a possibly-different program $p'$ and state $s'$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Configuration]
A \emph{configuration} is a pair $\cfg = (\prog, \state) \in P \times S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Run and halting]
The $n$-step run satisfies $\run(\cfg, 0) = \some\,\cfg$ and
\[
\run(\cfg, n{+}1) = \begin{cases} \none & \text{if } \step(\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state) = \none,\\ \run((p', s'), n) & \text{if } \step(\cdots) = \some(p', s'). \end{cases}
\]
The machine \emph{halts} from $\cfg$, written $\halts(\cfg)$, if $\run(\cfg, n) = \none$ for some $n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Standard machine]
A \emph{standard} machine over $S$ is a transition $\step : S \to \Opt S$, with run and $\halts(s) := \exists n,\ \run(s,n)=\none$ defined analogously; its program never changes.
\end{definition}

\section{The Simulation Theorem}\label{sec:sim}

\begin{definition}[Code becomes data]
For an SMM $m$ over $(P,S)$, define its standard simulation $m^{\mathrm{Std}}$ over $P \times S$ by
$m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\step(p,s) = \none$ if $m.\step(p,s) = \none$, and $\some(p',s')$ if $m.\step(p,s) = \some(p',s')$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Step-exact simulation]\label{lem:sim}
For every $\cfg$ and $n$,
\[
\big(m.\run(\cfg, n)\big).\mathrm{map}\,(c \mapsto (c.\prog, c.\state)) = m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\run\big((\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state),\, n\big).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$. The base case is immediate. For $n{+}1$, case on $m.\step(\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state)$: if $\none$ both sides are $\none$; if $\some(p',s')$ both reduce to the induction hypothesis at $(p',s')$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:none}
$m.\run(\cfg, n) = \none$ iff $m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\run((\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state), n) = \none$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Simulation Theorem]\label{thm:sim}
$m.\halts(\cfg) \iff m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\halts(\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Existential quantification over $n$ of the equivalence in Corollary~\ref{cor:none}.
\end{proof}

\section{Turing Equivalence of the Two Halting Problems}\label{sec:equiv}

\begin{theorem}[Self-modifying $\lemred$ standard]\label{thm:red1}
For any SMM $m$, $\ m.\halts \lemred m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\halts$ via $\cfg \mapsto (\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:sim}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Embedding]
For a standard machine $m$ over $S$, define $m^{\mathrm{emb}}$ over $(\mathrm{Unit}, S)$ by $m^{\mathrm{emb}}.\step(\_, s) = (m.\step(s)).\mathrm{map}\,(s' \mapsto ((), s'))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:emb}
$m^{\mathrm{emb}}.\run(((), s), n) = \none$ iff $m.\run(s, n) = \none$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$, casing on $m.\step(s)$; the $\mathrm{Unit}$ component is inert.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Standard $\lemred$ self-modifying]\label{thm:red2}
For any standard machine $m$, $\ m.\halts \lemred m^{\mathrm{emb}}.\halts$ via $s \mapsto ((), s)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Turing equivalence]\label{thm:equiv}
The self-modifying and standard halting problems are many-one equivalent. Self-modification does not make halting strictly harder.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:red1} and~\ref{thm:red2}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Decider transfer]\label{thm:decider}
If $D : P \times S \to \Bool$ decides $m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\halts$, then $D'(\cfg) := D(\cfg.\prog, \cfg.\state)$ decides $m.\halts$; conversely any decider for $m.\halts$ yields one for $m^{\mathrm{Std}}.\halts$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Both directions rewrite through Theorem~\ref{thm:sim}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The running program is absorbed into the data, so no strictly higher Turing degree is introduced. The undecidability is precisely the classical undecidability (degree $\mathbf{0}'$, level $\Sigma^0_1$).
\end{remark}

\section{The Self-Referential Halting Theorem}\label{sec:selfref}

\begin{theorem}[No correct decider]\label{thm:nocorrect}
Let $\Halts : \Prog \to \Prog \to \mathrm{Prop}$ and let $H : \Prog \to \Prog \to \Bool$ be any candidate decider. Suppose there is a contrarian program $d$ with
\begin{equation}\label{eq:contra}
\Halts(d, q) \iff H(q, q) = \false \qquad \text{for all } q.
\end{equation}
Then $H$ is not correct everywhere: $\lnot\big(H(d,d) = \true \iff \Halts(d,d)\big)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Set $q = d$ in \eqref{eq:contra}: $\Halts(d,d) \iff H(d,d) = \false$. If $H$ were correct at $d$, then $H(d,d) = \true \iff \Halts(d,d)$, giving $H(d,d) = \true \iff H(d,d) = \false$, impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Halting contradiction]\label{thm:contradiction}
There is no total decider $H$ that is correct everywhere ($H(p,q) = \true \iff \Halts(p,q)$) while the system admits the contrarian $d$ of \eqref{eq:contra}.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:nocorrect} exhibits $d$ as a point of incorrectness.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]\label{prop:nonvac}
The hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:nocorrect} are satisfiable: take $\Prog = \mathbb{N}$, $\Halts(p,q) := (p \ne 0)$, $H \equiv \true$, $d = 0$; then \eqref{eq:contra} holds since both sides are false.
\end{proposition}

\section{The Virus Paradox and the Alignment Obstruction}\label{sec:apps}

\begin{theorem}[Virus paradox]\label{thm:virus}
Let $\mathrm{Detect} : \Prog \to \Bool$ be total, and suppose a contrarian $d$ satisfies $\Halts(d, q) \iff \mathrm{Detect}(q) = \false$ for all $q$. Then it is not the case that $\mathrm{Detect}(q) = \true \iff \Halts(q, q)$ for all $q$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:nocorrect} with $H(p,q) := \mathrm{Detect}(q)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Alignment obstruction]\label{thm:align}
Let $M : \Prog \to \Bool$ be a total safety monitor, $M(q) = \true$ intended to certify $\lnot\,\Halts(q,q)$. Suppose a contrarian $d$ satisfies
\begin{equation}\label{eq:align}
\Halts(d, q) \iff M(q) = \true \qquad \text{for all } q.
\end{equation}
Then $M$ is wrong on some program: there exists $q$ with $\lnot\big(M(q) = \true \iff \lnot\,\Halts(q, q)\big)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $q = d$. From \eqref{eq:align}, $\Halts(d,d) \iff M(d) = \true$. If $M$ were correct at $d$, then $M(d) = \true \iff \lnot\,\Halts(d,d)$. Chaining, with $P := \Halts(d,d)$, gives $P \iff \lnot P$; then $\lnot P$ holds, and $\lnot P$ gives $P$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

No total monitor can correctly certify a non-trivial self-referential behavioral property for every program: an oversight mechanism that is total, sound, and universal is impossible for a sufficiently expressive agent class.

\section{A Worked Example}

Consider a self-modifying machine with programs $P = \{A, B\}$, state $S = \mathbb{N}$, and transition $\step(p, s) = \none$ if $s = 0$ and $\step(p, s) = (\overline{p}, s - 1)$ if $s > 0$, where $\overline{A} = B$, $\overline{B} = A$. The run from $(A,3)$ visits $(A,3) \to (B,2) \to (A,1) \to (B,0) \to \none$: the program in control alternates every step, yet the machine is well-behaved. Its simulation $m^{\mathrm{Std}}$ over $P \times S$ (``read $(p,s)$, decrement $s$, flip $p$'') produces the identical sequence of pairs, so Theorem~\ref{thm:sim} is visible directly, and halting is trivially decidable (it halts after exactly $s$ steps) because the enlarged state exposes the counter as data. By contrast, the undecidability of Sections~\ref{sec:selfref}--\ref{sec:apps} arises not from rewriting but from diagonalization against a predictor: the contrarian $d$'s changes depend on a verdict about $d$'s own code. That closed loop---description, prediction, negation---not self-modification per se, is what no analyzer escapes.

\section{Discussion}

\emph{Self-modification is computationally free.} By the Simulation Theorem and Turing equivalence, a self-rewriting machine is exactly as powerful---and its halting problem exactly as hard---as a fixed-program machine over a larger state. \emph{Self-reference is the real wall.} The impossibility comes from a system's ability to describe a proposed analyzer and act against it, the diagonal argument of Cantor and Turing, traced to Lawvere via the single fixed-point-free map $\lnot$. In AI-alignment terms, a perfect, always-correct, universal safety monitor is provably impossible against sufficiently expressive agents; viable oversight must relax totality, soundness, or universality.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{A concrete universal contrarian.} Build $d$ explicitly inside a concrete universal SMM over $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$, making the undecidability unconditional.
  \item \textbf{Oracle self-modification and the arithmetical hierarchy.} Add oracle access and prove the halting problem stays $\Sigma^0_1$ relative to the oracle, with non-collapse for iterated oracles.
  \item \textbf{Bounded self-modification depth.} Machines rewriting at most $k$ times: decidable at $k=0$, undecidable for $k \ge 1$ over a Turing-complete base.
  \item \textbf{Rice's theorem in full.} Generalize the virus paradox to every non-trivial behavioral property of the self-modifying run.
  \item \textbf{Quantitative alignment.} A measure-theoretic or resource-bounded strengthening of the alignment obstruction bounding how often any monitor must err.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Self-modifying code cannot be perfectly predicted---but not because it is strictly harder than ordinary code. It is many-one equivalent to the classical halting problem: code that rewrites itself is code that shuffles data. The true obstruction is self-reference: any system able to build a contrarian defeats every candidate predictor of its own behavior, yielding the virus paradox and a computability-theoretic wall for AI alignment.

